<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.PlanFooter"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        THE ASK AT THE FOOT OF A PLAN SHEET: what stands in the way, what has to be typed, and the
        two buttons.

        IT LEFT PlanView.xaml ON 2026-09-07 AND THE SIZE RATCHET IS WHAT ASKED - that file stood on
        EXACTLY the markup ceiling of 376 lines, so the confirmation box could not be added until
        something came out. What came out is a subject rather than a block of lines: everything
        above the rule is EVIDENCE and it scrolls, everything here is the ASK and it never does.

        THAT DIVISION IS ALSO THE REASON THE CONFIRMATION BOX IS HERE RATHER THAN BESIDE THE STEPS
        IT IS ABOUT. A body that scrolls can be left unread - somebody can reach the button without
        ever meeting a control halfway up it - and a confirmation somebody can skip past is not one.
        The footer is the only part of this sheet that is on the screen whatever else is.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl is a ContentControl and arrives with
        both - `docs/10` trap 8, which is the one that adds a silent extra Tab stop every time a
        piece of this window is carried out to a file of its own.
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <BooleanToVisibilityConverter x:Key="WhenTrue" />
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!--
        THE RULE ABOVE IT IS WHY THE BUTTON STOPPED LOOKING ORPHANED. It sits at the bottom rather
        than under the steps - `docs/11` 9.2 wants the effect shown before the ask, and a way to say
        yes that has to be scrolled to is one somebody presses without reaching the end.

        INTERRUPT IS A SEPARATE BUTTON RATHER THAN THE OTHER ONE CHANGING ITS WORD, and that is
        safety: a verb that changes under a finger turns a double click into an instruction nobody
        gave. It appears to the LEFT, so the rectangle somebody aimed at never becomes something
        else - it becomes disabled.
    -->
    <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}"
            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderFooterRule}"
            Padding="{StaticResource PaddingFooter}"
            Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
        <!--
            AN ADORNER LAYER OF THE FOOTER'S OWN, 2026-09-16. The wrong box's edge is the error
            template of its style, and an error template is drawn in the nearest adorner layer
            above the box. The window has one, in its template - and a test host never applies a
            window's template, so a render of the sheet alone, which is how WaitingBoxGuards looks
            at it, would show three red sides and the library's own line along the bottom, which
            is the picture the owner sent. With the layer here the edge is the footer's to draw
            and to render: on screen the nearest layer wins and nothing looks different.
        -->
        <AdornerDecorator>
        <StackPanel>
            <!--
                WHY IT CANNOT BE CARRIED OUT, MOVED DOWN HERE ON 2026-09-01 FROM THE TOP OF THE
                PANEL. The owner's decision of 2026-08-18 stands unchanged - it is said BEFORE
                anybody presses anything - and what changes is that it is now said beside the
                thing it is about. At the top it was a second warning under a title, three
                scrolling sections away from the grey button it explains.

                It only appears when something is in the way, so no empty line is ever reserved
                for reassurance.

                A BLOCK WITH A WARNING-COLOURED RULE SINCE 2026-09-02, and the rule is all that
                is added: the sentence keeps its words, its colour and its own Visibility. Loose
                orange prose across a panel reads as damage, and this is not damage - it is the
                reason the button under it will refuse. THE BORDER REPEATS THE BINDING RATHER
                THAN TAKING IT: moving it up would leave the TextBlock permanently visible, and
                three guards read that exact property to say whether the window admits it
                cannot act.
            -->
            <Border Style="{StaticResource PanelBlockRefused}"
                    Visibility="{Binding HasBlocked, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
                <TextBlock x:Name="PlanBlocked"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planBlocked"
                           Text="{Binding Blocked}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Visibility="{Binding HasBlocked, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}"
                           Style="{StaticResource ProblemText}" />
            </Border>

            <!--
                THE HEAVIEST CONFIRMATION THIS PROJECT HAS, AND IT IS ALMOST NEVER ON THE SCREEN.
                `docs/11` 9.2 point 2 puts the weight of an ask on the size of what it does, so this
                appears only where ending a process takes down more than the entry somebody named,
                or takes down one the machine will not run without. Measured 2026-08-01: 105 of 110
                processes on this machine host exactly one service, so the ordinary forcing plan
                shows the footer that shipped and nothing more.

                THE WHOLE BLOCK GOES WHEN IT IS NOT WANTED, heading included - backlog 203, which
                this window has already paid for once. A label reading "Type Spooler to confirm"
                over a plan that never asked for it would leave the button grey with nothing on
                screen explaining what would turn it on.

                UpdateSourceTrigger IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. A TextBox tells its binding on losing
                focus by default, so without this line the name would be typed, the button would
                stay grey, and it would come alive only when somebody clicked somewhere else -
                which reads exactly like a confirmation that does not work.
            -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="ConfirmSection"
                        Visibility="{Binding NeedsTyping, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
                <!--
                    WHY THE NAME HAS TO BE TYPED, OVER THE BOX IT HAS TO BE TYPED INTO, since
                    2026-09-16. The sentence saying the machine would go down used to stand in
                    "Worth knowing" - in the half of the sheet that scrolls, in plain white beside
                    "it will be back after the next restart" - and the label under it said only
                    "Type PlugPlay to confirm". The owner's words: the person does not know what
                    they are confirming. The argument at the head of this file for putting the box
                    in the footer - a confirmation somebody can skip past is not one - is the
                    argument for its reason standing here too, and it leaves "Worth knowing" rather
                    than being said twice.

                    THE BLOCK IS THE REFUSED ONE, NOT A THIRD. Its red rule has meant "stop and read
                    this" since 2026-09-02, and a block of its own in the same red would be a second
                    name for one look. The text style is the one place this sheet weighs a sentence:
                    DangerText, in Text.xaml, with the measurement beside it.
                -->
                <Border Style="{StaticResource PanelBlockRefused}"
                        Visibility="{Binding HasDanger, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
                    <TextBlock x:Name="PlanDanger"
                               AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planDanger"
                               Text="{Binding Danger}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               Style="{StaticResource DangerText}" />
                </Border>

                <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planConfirmLabel"
                           Text="{Binding TypeToConfirm}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

                <TextBox x:Name="ConfirmBox"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planConfirm"
                         AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding TypeToConfirm}"
                         ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.confirm.hint}"
                         Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}"
                         Text="{Binding Typed, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <!--
                TWO ROWS SINCE 2026-09-15: the controls, and under them the one sentence the box of
                seconds can say about itself. THE SECOND ROW IS ALWAYS THERE, empty or not - an empty
                TextBlock keeps the height of one line, measured at 16 for the small size - because
                this row holds the button somebody is aiming at. A sentence that appeared only when
                needed would push nothing (it is below the button) but would grow the sheet, and a
                centred sheet growing moves everything in it by half a line. GUI rule 3: nothing on
                this sheet may jump under a hand.
            -->
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <!--
                    HOW LONG ONE STEP MAY BE WATCHED FOR, WHICH THE WINDOW HAD NO WAY OF SAYING UNTIL
                    2026-09-09 - backlog 330, and the pre-release audit's RELEASE-008. The terminal has
                    had a timeout switch since it could stop anything, so "wait longer" was an answer available
                    to one of two interfaces, and somebody here whose service needed ninety seconds
                    could only watch it be abandoned at sixty.

                    THE WHOLE ROW GOES WHEN THE PLAN HAS NOTHING TO WAIT FOR, which is backlog 203 for
                    the second time in this file: a start type change writes a setting and moves no
                    service, so a box offering to change how long it is watched for would be a control
                    that does nothing on a sheet where everything else does something.

                    A PLAIN TEXTBOX RATHER THAN A NUMBER BOX WITH ARROWS. What somebody types here is a
                    number they already know - the service they have learnt is slow - rather than one
                    they arrive at by nudging, and the arrows would be two more Tab stops between the
                    plan and the button that carries it out.

                    IT HOLDS TEXT, NOT A NUMBER, SINCE 2026-09-15 - point 5 of the review in `docs/11`
                    2.14. Bound to an int, the box let WPF do the reading and WPF reads in silence:
                    a word was a conversion error nobody saw, zero was refused by a setter that said
                    nothing, and the run used sixty under a box reading "abc". The model reads the
                    text with the terminal's own rule and says under the box when it is not a number
                    of seconds - WaitingBox is the style that colours the edge while it says so, and
                    the button greys with the same reason on it.

                    UpdateSourceTrigger for the same reason the confirmation box has it: without it the
                    number would be typed and the run would still use the old one until somebody
                    clicked elsewhere, which is a disagreement between the screen and the machine.

                    THE WIDTH IS A NAME SINCE 2026-09-15 and was a raw 56 here - backlog 352, GUI
                    rule 12. The number did not move, only where it lives.
                -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="WaitingSection"
                            Grid.Column="0"
                            Grid.Row="0"
                            Orientation="Horizontal"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.waiting.hint}"
                            Visibility="{Binding Waits, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
                    <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.plan.waiting.label}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

                    <TextBox x:Name="WaitingBox"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planWaiting"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.plan.waiting.label}"
                             Style="{StaticResource WaitingBox}"
                             Width="{StaticResource WidthWaitingBox}"
                             Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                             TextAlignment="Right"
                             Text="{Binding WaitingText, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}" />

                    <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.plan.waiting.unit}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!--
                    WHAT IS WRONG WITH THE BOX, UNDER THE ROW IT IS IN. Empty almost always, and
                    ALWAYS PRESENT - the row it sits in is reserved, see the head of this grid. In
                    the problem colour, the same one the box's edge wears, so the sentence and the
                    field read as one thing. Spans every column because it is a sentence rather than
                    a control, and a sentence wrapping inside the first column would be a tall
                    narrow paragraph beside two buttons.
                -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                           Grid.Row="1"
                           x:Name="WaitingProblemLine"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planWaitingProblem"
                           Text="{Binding WaitingProblem}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Style="{StaticResource ProblemText}" />

                <!--
                    IT SHARES ITS CELL WITH THE BOX OF SECONDS AND IS DECLARED AFTER IT, SO IT LIES
                    ON TOP - and until 2026-09-16 it lay there at all times, empty. A TextBlock takes
                    the pointer over the whole of its rectangle, text or no text, so this line,
                    stretched across the column, caught every click meant for the box under it: the
                    owner could not type into the box on a start or restart plan, and the keyboard
                    stayed on the close button. Measured by tools/gui-probe/plan-keys.ps1, settled by
                    a hit test in WaitingBoxGuards. It goes when it has nothing to say - the rule the
                    row beside it already follows - which is a repair of the cause rather than of the
                    symptom (IsHitTestVisible would leave an invisible element on top of a control).
                -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           x:Name="PlanProgress"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planProgress"
                           Text="{Binding Progress}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Visibility="{Binding HasProgress, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}"
                           Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        x:Name="InterruptButton"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planInterrupt"
                        Content="{DynamicResource gui.plan.interrupt}"
                        ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.interrupt.hint}"
                        Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                        Visibility="{Binding Busy, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}"
                        Click="InterruptRequested" />

                <!--
                    THE TOOLTIP SAYS WHY IT IS GREY, AND ShowOnDisabled MAKES IT REACHABLE AT
                    ALL: WPF stops serving tooltips for a disabled control, so without this line
                    the reason below is a sentence nobody could read.

                    Bound rather than a fixed key, because the button has FIVE ways of going quiet
                    and only one was ever said out loud - CarryOutTip chooses between them. The
                    fifth arrived with the box above it and is the only one somebody can clear
                    from where they are standing.

                    ITS CONTENT IS BOUND TOO, SINCE 2026-09-07, AND SINCE 2026-09-15 IT NAMES THE
                    ACT ON EVERY PLAN. "End process 4812" was the owner's decision of 2026-09-07
                    for a forcing plan, with an argument that was already general - "Carry this
                    out" describes a plan where the press does something - and the review of
                    2026-09-15 (`docs/11` 2.14, point 4) applied it to the rest: "Stop Windows
                    Search", "Restart 3 entries", "Set Spooler to Disabled".

                    A NAME IS UNBOUNDED AND THIS ROW IS NOT. The button stops growing at
                    WidthCarryOutMost and its word is trimmed with an ellipsis through
                    TrimmedButtonLabel, so the longest display name on a Polish Windows cannot push
                    the waiting box off the row - the heading above carries the whole name. The
                    automation name is bound as well as the content, so a probe reading this button
                    gets the label whichever way WPF derives a name for templated content.
                -->
                <Button Grid.Column="2"
                        x:Name="CarryOutButton"
                        Style="{StaticResource DestructiveAction}"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planCarryOut"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding CarryOutLabel}"
                        Content="{Binding CarryOutLabel}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TrimmedButtonLabel}"
                        MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthCarryOutMost}"
                        ToolTip="{Binding CarryOutTip}"
                        ToolTipService.ShowOnDisabled="True"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanCarryOut}"
                        Click="CarryOutRequested" />
            </Grid>
        </StackPanel>
        </AdornerDecorator>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
